A||||9||mrfiero||interesting problem w/my GT||||11||
Z||000000||mrfiero||06-13-2001||01:04 AM||||I have had an intermittant problem with my "new" '88 GT.  After driving it for a long time (city type driving) it would die......almost felt like it was vapor locking, as if it were starving for gas.  The fuel pump would buzz like crazy, but it wouldn't run.<P>I checked the schraeder valve and there was plenty of fuel getting to the rail, so I was stumped.  If I let it cool off a bit it would start and run OK......same thing with short trips.  <P>I completely tuned it up and thought I had it fixed, but today, after lots if stop and go driving, it died again.  Here's what I discovered.......the return line from the fuel rail (where the solid line turns to rubber) was soft and spongy.  Evidently the battery has been leaking directly onto the hose and when the engine compartment gets really hot the hose restricts so much that the excess fuel can't flow back into the tank.  This causes a backup and an "over pressurized" system (which is why the fuel pump would go crazy).  <P>Luckily it's an easy fix, I just need to replace the hose.  I just wanted to share this experience with you all.  I know I have never had this happen before, nor have I ever heard of it happening to anyone else.   <P>What's next?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000001||stimpy||06-13-2001||10:05 AM||||Money permitting I'd replace that leakiy battery with a spiral core type. like an Optima or Exide Orbital. Great battery, and they will never leak.||||reg||1
Z||000002||thomas_l||06-13-2001||05:53 PM||||Check it with a fuel pressure gauge. You could get 5 psi at the schraeder and have lots of gas coming out, but the engine wouldn't run.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||06-13-2001||07:45 PM||||I plan on getting a new battery.....thanks Stimpy.<P>Low fuel pressure wasn't/isn't my problem......it was too much that caused my woes.  It runs and drives great (I drove it today with no problems), it's just when it gets really hot the bad hose constricts enough to prohibit the excess gas from the fuel rail to make its way back to the tank.<P>This in turn causes too much fuel pressure at the rail......so much that it would choke the engine (flood it out) and cause it to die.  I could make it 2 blocks before it would stall......I would then get out and bleed off the excess pressure (with a screwdriver and a towel).....make it another two blocks and so on.  I did this for about a mile until I shut it down and had a few beers and played some pool at a local bar for a few hours.  When I left it had cooled down enough to make it the rest of the way home (about 25 miles).  You wouldn't believe how much/how high the pressure was.....I wish I did have a gauge at the time.....I'd like to see what it read.        <P>I will take a picture of the hose when I remove it, so everyone can see what I'm talking about.<P>Thanks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||06-13-2001||11:34 PM||||I wonder if maybe someone had replaced the hose, and instead of the proper neopreem, used something else, rubber or whatever. I know if you use a rubber hose for a gas line it will get all weird on you.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||06-14-2001||12:36 AM||||It's a factory hose.....it's just badly deteriorated by the battery acid (I have no idea how long it's been leaking into the hose).  ||||reg||7
Z||000006||StuGood||06-14-2001||07:46 AM||||Thanks for sharing your experience!  If that ever happens to my car, I just hope I'll remember what you said here.<P>Too <B>much</B> fuel pressure probably would have been my <B>last</B> guess.  And too much fuel pressure due to a deteriorated return hose?  I bet I would never have thought of it.<P>As these cars get up in years, these sorts of "weird" problems will undoubtedly occur.   That's why sharing each others' experiences is so important now, and will be even more so in the future.  Thanks again for sharing.       ||||reg||6
Z||000007||Alex4mula||06-14-2001||11:31 AM||||High fuel preasure?? Add a 100hp shot NOS bottle & probelm solve!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||06-14-2001||02:22 PM||||Stu,<P>I would never have thought of it either, but when it died I popped the decklid to look around.  I saw a darker area on one of the hoses and thought I had a fuel leak (hose was more black in one area).  When I put my hand down there to feel the hose (to see if there was gas on it) I got a handful of rubber.  The hose was falling apart in my hand!<P><BR>Alex,<P>I think something would have popped if I were running nitrous when this happened.  Now that would have been a sight to see!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ronen||06-14-2001||05:00 PM||||The exact same thing happened to me. It was the transmission selunoid (sp?), it was stuck. The fix is cheap and quick.||||reg||1
